asure this  nanoscale crystallization temperature in  phase change memory devices  so stepping back for a minute and  starting to think a bit more broadly  about these examples that i've just  discussed there are certain recurring  themes that start to emerge in terms of  the types of systems where we'd like to  be able to measure temperature at the  nanoscale  and so one theme is that we'd like to be  able to measure the temperature across  stacks of thin films whether that's in  the active region of leds or other  optoelectronic devices or in the  recording medium and heatsink layers  underneath the hammer peg  another theme is that we'd like to be  able to measure the temperature of  nanoscale hotspots  like the hammer peg itself or the  unknown crystallization temperature in  these phase change memory devices  and so today i'll focus on the nano  scale hotspot since that's really the  particular application that the  technique i'll be talking about today is  best suited to address  before getting into some of the  experimental techniques  i want to discuss some of the  more fundamental motivation for  nanoscale thermometry techniques and  what i mean by that is we might ask you  know couldn't we just develop very  sophisticated models or calculations in  order  to determine what the temperature of  these nanoscale hotspots could be  it turns out that one major challenge  here is that the classical laws of heat  transfer that say you might learn in an  undergraduate course don't always hold  up at the nanoscale  and we can understand that by thinking  about the example of a heat generating  sphere in an infinite medium  so if we zoom into this medium what we'd  see is that there are heat carriers say  phonons that are running around and  colliding with one another  and in between collisions they travel an  average distance that we call the mean  street path or lambda  and in the classical fourier's law case  that again you might see in an  undergraduate course the diameter d of  the sphere is much larger than the mean  free path lambda of these heat carriers  and so we can then use this kinetic  theory expression for thermal  conductivity k that says k equals 1 3 cv  lambda and we can use this expression  along with fourier's law to calculate  the temperature rise of the sphere  but now let's consider a different case  where we have a heap generating  nanosphere whose diameter d is much much  smaller than that mean free path lambda  it turns out that in this case which we  call a ballistic heat transfer scenario  fourier's law no longer applies and  instead we have to solve the boltzmann  transport equation in order to calculate  the temperature rise of our sphere  and in this simple case we can get an  analytical solution and it turns out  that when the sphere is much smaller  than that mean free path lambda the  temperature rise is much much larger  than what fourier's law predicts  and so we can expect that in other more  complicated nanoscale geometries and  devices we're again going to have  deviations from this classical fourier  heat transfer scenario  but we don't always know the details and  so we really need experimental tools in  order to characterize and understand  these kinds of nanoscale subcontinuum  effects  starting to think in more detail about  thermometry methods that already exist  we can divide them into two broad  categories the first of which is  non-contact optical methods  and here one example that may be quite  familiar is infrared thermal imaging and  this is a very convenient approach but  the spatial resolution of this kind of  technique is limited to about 10 microns  near room temperature due to the long  wavelength of thermal radiation  and you can do a bit better by going to  high temperatures which is something  that i've done along with some  collaborators previously  but even at 3000 kelvin your spatial  resolution is still limited to about a  micron and so you just cannot get the  reso