n of these two excited states  is governed by boltzmann statistics and  that's fundamentally where our  temperature dependent signal comes from  the relative  populations of these two excited states  it turns out are directly related to the  emission intensities  from the two corresponding transitions  and we can see that by looking at single  particle spectra taken at different  temperatures  so if we divide the emission wavelength  range into two bands lambda 1 to lambda  2 and lambda 2 to lambda 3  we see that when we increase the  temperature  the emission intensity from this first  wavelength band which corresponds to the  transition from the higher energy  excited state to the ground state  increases relative to the emission  intensity from the second band which  corresponds to the transition from the  lower energy excited state to the ground  state  we can then define a temperature  dependent ratio as the integrated  emission intensity from the first  wavelength band divided by the  integrated intensity from the second  band  and this is an approach known as  ratiometric thermometry  it's been used quite commonly for  ensembles or large groups of these  upconverting nanoparticles but prior to  our work a single nanoparticle  measurements were quite rare and there  were none that use single particles  under 50 nanometers  next i'll just discuss briefly how we  perform these measurements and then show  some of the results  so we perform these measurements using a  custom scanning confocal microscopy  system and we begin by focusing a  near-infrared laser using a high n a  objective and this focused laser beam  then excites a single nanoparticle which  sits on both a  piezo control nano positioning stage and  a temperature controlled thermal stage  we then collect the emitted luminescence  filter out any residual excitation light  and then we can send that emitted  luminescence either to an avalanche  photodiode for imaging or to a  spectrometer with a ccd camer