n intensity  we saw something that was different than  what we expected  so here you see the ratio measured as a  function of excitation intensity  where our substrate temperature is fixed  at room temperature about 296 kelvin  and we see that as we increase the  excitation intensity that temperature  dependent ratio also increases  likewise if we park our thermal stage at  a higher temperature of 350 kelvin and  we again increase the excitation ratio  uh the excitation intensity  we once again see that this temperature  dependent ratio increases  so what we can then do is to convert  this change in the ratio with excitation  intensity to an apparent temperature  rise  and if we do that we get an apparent  temperature rise  of over 50 kelvin so much much higher  than our worst case scenario estimate of  just 2 kelvin  and from this data we can also extract  an apparent thermal resistance and when  we do that we get a value of about 6.3  times 10 to the 10 kelvins per watt  so again more than an order of magnitude  higher than the worst case scenario  modeled value  and so it's really this dramatic  discrepancy between model and experiment  that's motivated the next set of  experiments we performed to try and  answer the question of could the  nanoparticle really be heating and if so  how can we explain this  so after observing this unexpectedly  large apparent temperature rise one of  our first thoughts was to next try to  use a second thermometry method  to verify  uh this large temperature rise and so  for the sake of time i won't go into  details but we developed a second  thermometry method based on the  temperature dependent luminescence  lifetime of these nanoparticles  and so we use this method to once again  measure the apparent temperature rise as  a function of excitation intensity  and once again we got a quite large  apparent temperature rise of somewhere  around 50 kelvin and so the second  thermometry method seemed to confirm  that the nanoparticle was