le one of these  nanoparticles and place it at the exact  location on the sample where we want to  measure temperature that's of course  very critical because if we don't have a  way to do that then this whole technique  really falls apart  and so the approach that we take here is  that we use an atomic force microscope  tip or afn tip  to push individual nanoparticles around  substrates and place them at the  location where we'd like to measure  temperature  so the way that we do that and a lot of  this work right now is being done by my  graduate student dinesh committee  is that we essentially hack the nano  lithography mode on our park nx10 afm  and then we use that to position  individual nanoparticles uh on various  solid substrates  and so the examples that i'll show here  are for  individual  nanodiamonds  that are about 100 nanometers or even  less in diameter but again we apply this  technique quite broadly to all kinds of  nanoparticles  uh so we can do both microscale  manipulation which is how dinesh made  this r for rochester that you see shown  here  and then we can also do finer movements  so here's an example where we move one  particle with respect to another  by about 95 nanometers  and then here's another example of  moving a single particle by just about  20 nanometers so this provides a  powerful way to place single  nanoparticles exactly where we want to  perform our temperature measurements  so to wrap up in my group we really  focus on pushing the boundaries of  far-field optical nanothermometry  uh in the past we've done a lot of work  on using individual upconverting  nanoparticles for sub 50 nanometer  single point thermometry and that's  really the majority of the work that i  talked about today  uh and then we have some other projects  in slightly different directions at the  moment  finally i'd like to acknowledge uh past  funding support from uc berkeley and the  nsf  uh the molecular foundry at lawrence  berkeley lab and seagate and then  current funding support from the  university of rochester and the american  chemical society petroleum research fund  um if you're interested in learning more  please feel free to visit our website  listed here at the bottom of the slide  and then with that i'd be happy to  answer any questions  thank you andrea for your informative  presentation we'll now start the live q  a portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on that ask a question  box located on the far left of your  screen we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  let's get started  our first question  um how do the up converting  nanoparticles compare to other  fluorescent therm thermometers  uh sure so that's a great question  because certainly these upconverting  nanoparticles are not the only types of  luminescent materials that have been  used for thermometry  there's a lot of things like fluorescent  dyes proteins that have been used for  thermometry as well as other inorganic  materials  so for example nitrogen vacancy centers  and nanodiamonds and we work with some  of these other materials as well  so i think some of the major advantages  of the upconverting nanoparticle would  be first of all they're just extremely  uniform  and i talked about that a bit when i  showed some of these ratio metric  thermometry's results for different  individual particles and you see that  these calibration curves are really  uniform  from one particle to another so that's a  big advantage in terms of being able to  do say a batch calibration where you  calibrate a small number of single  particles  from a batch and then you can go on and  apply that calibration to a totally  different nanoparticle from that same  batch  so that's one big advantage  another  aspect that i'd highlight is  because you excite these upconverted  nanoparticles in the near ir but then  they emit  hundreds of nanometers away in the  visible wavelength range that can be a  big advantage if you're trying to avoid  a spectral overlap with some other kind  of signal  and that's such a huge anti-stokes shift  that you really don't get from a lot of  other types of materials  and so that can be  you know a unique advantage as well  thank you for that andreas our next  question  if you were to use a single up  converting nanoparticle as a thermometer  in a plasmonic environment such as the  hammer hard drive we mentioned would you  expect expect this plasmonic environment  to change the ratiometric thermometry  signal  sure so yeah that's an important  question right because we know that for  something like that hammer hard drive  application you're going to need to  put these nanoparticles in a very  intense plasmonic environment and it's  definitely reasonable  to worry that you know perhaps this  plasmonic environment could change the  temperature dependent luminescence  signal  and so one thing that's helpful is that  there are other people who have already  used these upconverting nanoparticles  for thermometry in plasmonic  environments  and they actually see that  the ratio values that you get as a  function of temperature  are extremely similar to what you get in  more of a conventional non-plasmonic  environment  so that's kind of a helpful precedent  there  you get some enhancement in the overall  brightness which is actually  a big advantage perhaps but you don't  see distortions  of the ratio  the other thing that can be helpful and  this goes back to what i was saying  previously about having this advantage  of a large separation between  the excitation wavelength and the  emission wavelength and then also the  fact that these wavelengths are just  very tunable  uh by changing these lanthanide dopants  is that you can  basically ensure that your excitation  wavelengths and your emission  wavelengths are both not anywhere near  the plasmonic resonance  and so that's something that you know we  were thinking about when