ransistors now have feature  sizes on the order of tens of nanometers  and so hot spots have shrunk to the  nanoscale as well  another area where heat transfer is  important is in a number of energy  applications and here one particularly  important example is lighting which  accounts for about six percent of our  total energy use here in the us  and as we've shifted away from  incandescent light bulbs to leds the  thermal challenges associated with  lighting have shifted to the nanoscale  and that's because leds are  semiconductor devices that contain many  thin layers some of which are only a few  nanometers thick  and although leds are a lot more  efficient than incandescent bulbs they  still waste typically at least 10 of  their input energy as heat and an led  operating above its maximum rate of  temperature can suffer huge performance  and reliability losses  even a 10 degree increase can cut the  lifetime of an led in half  on the other hand there are also  applications where rather than being  parasitic localized heating can actually  be desirable if it's carefully  controlled  one example here is thermal cancer  therapies where nanoparticles are  embedded in cancerous cells and then a  laser is used to selectively heat the  nanoparticles with the goal of then  killing the cancerous cells by heating  them while leaving the healthy tissue  intact  another place where controlled localized  heating can be beneficial is in some  next generation data storage  technologies one example here is heat  assisted magnetic recording where  localized heating is used to temporarily  change the magnetic properties of the  recording medium and another example is  phase change memory where joule heating  is used to switch a nanoscale amount of  phase change material  between two different states  and so next to understand how  specifically the inability to measure  temperature limits some of these  technologies i'll discuss a couple  examples in more detail  key to cystic magnetic recording or  hammer is as i said a next generation  hard drive technology and it's expected  to more than double storage densities  the key idea behind hammer is to  plasmonically excite this gold  lollipop shaped structure known as the  near field transducer and then couple  energy from the 30 nanometer peg across  across a nanoscale gas and into the  recording medium below  and this energy then locally heats the  recording medium which temporarily and  lowers its coercive coercivity and  ultimately allows for smaller bit size  and higher storage densities  but one challenge for hammer is that  it's known that rather than heating the  recording medium instead significant  overheating of the 30 nanometer peg can  occur  and degradation of this peg can cause  entire hammer drives to fail  and so again  it's thought that the cause of this  degradation is overheating right now  there's no existing technique that can  measure the temperature of the hammer  peg during operation to verify whether  this is actually the case  phase change memory is another next  generation data storage technology and  here the idea is to use joule heating to  switch a nanoscale amount of a phase  change material typically a calcagenide  glass between a resistive amorphous  state and a conductive crystalline state  and the way that this works is that to  go from crystalline to amorphous the  phase change material is heated above  its melting temperature and then quickly  quench to lock in the amorphous state  and to go from amorphous to crystalline  the phase change material is heated  above what's known as the  crystallization temperature which is  lower than the melting temperature  and then held there until it  recrystallizes  it's known that the crystallization  temperature of these nanoscale amounts  of phase change material can differ  significantly from the bulk value  some estimate suggests by hundreds of  degrees  but again right now there's no existing  technique that can measure this  nanoscale crystallization temperature in  phase change memory devices  so stepping back for a minute and  starting to think a bit more broadly  about these examples that i've just  discussed there are certain recurring  themes that start to emerge in terms of  the types of systems where we'd like to  be able to measure temperature at the  nanoscale  and so one theme is that we'd like to be  able to measure the temperature across  stacks of thin films whether that's in  the active region of leds or other  optoelectronic devices or in the  recording medium and heatsink layers  underneath the hammer peg  another theme is that we'd like to be  able to measure the temperature of  nanoscale hotspots  like the hammer peg itself or the  unknown crystallization temperature in  these phase change memory devices  and so today i'll focus on the nano  scale hotspot since that's really the  particular application that the  technique i'll be talking about today is  best suited to address  before getting into some of the  experimental techniques  i want to discuss some of the  more fundamental motivation for  nanoscale thermometry techniques and  what i mean by that is we might ask you  know couldn't we just develop very  sophisticated models or calculations in  order  to determine what the temperature of  these nanoscale hotspots could be  it turns out that one major challenge  here is that the classical laws of heat  transfer that say you might learn in an  undergraduate course don't always hold  up at the nanoscale  and we can understand that by thinking  about the example of a heat generating  sphere in an infinite medium  so if we zoom into this medium what we'd  see is that there are heat carriers say  phonons that are running around and  colliding with one another  and in between collisions they travel an  average distance that we call the mean  street path or lambda  and in the classical fourier's law case  that again you might see in an  undergraduate course the diameter d of  the sphere is much larger than the mean  free path lambda of these heat carriers  and so we can then use this kinetic  theory expression for thermal  conductivity k that says k equals 1 3 cv  lambda and we can use this expression  along with fourier's law to calculate  the temperature rise of the sphere  but now let's consider a different case  where we have a heap generating  nanosphere whose diameter d is much much  smaller than that mean free path lambda  it turns out that in this case which we  call a ballistic heat transfer scenario  fourier's law no longer applies and  instead we have to solve the boltzmann  transport equation in order to calculate  the temperature rise of our sphere  and in this simple case we can get an  analytical solution and it turns out  that when the sphere is much smaller  than that mean free path lambda the  temperature rise is much much larger  than what fourier's law predicts  and so we can expect that in other more  complicated nanoscale geometries and  devices we're again going to have  deviations from this classical fourier  heat transfer scenario  but we don't always know the details and  so we really need experimental tools in  order to characterize and understand  these kinds of nanoscale subcontinuum  effects  starting to think in more detail about  thermometry methods that already exist  we can divide them into two broad  categories the first of which is  non-contact optical methods  and here one example that may be quite  familiar is infrared thermal imaging and  this is a very convenient approach but  the spatial resolution of this kind of  technique is limited to about 10 microns  near room temperature due to the long  wavelength of thermal radiation  and you can do a bit better by going to  high temperatures which is something  that i've done along with some  collaborators previously  but even at 3000 kelvin your spatial  resolution is still limited to about a  micron and so you just cannot get the  resolution that you need in order to  measure nanoscale hot spots  there are also many laser-based  techniques uh one common example here is  thermal reflectance techniques that use  the temperature dependent optical  reflectivity of a thin metal transducer  layer  for thermometry and here you can get  spatial resolution down to a few hundred  nanometers so that's a very fundamental  limit due to optical diffraction  and so to get the spatial resolution  that we need to measure nanoscale  hotspots we might instead think about  contact-based techniques for example  scanning probe techniques that  essentially use a modified afm tip as a  nanoscale from a couple  and here you can get quite good spatial  resolution but the challenge is that the  probe can act as a parasitic heat sink  and introduce unknown thermal contact  resistances and so it can be very  difficult to accurately extract the true  temperature of the sample that you're  measuring  uh that said as convenient as the  non-contact techniques are they are  fundamentally diffraction limited as i  mentioned  and so the question that we like to ask  in my group is  is there some way that we can still use  far-field optics but somehow beat the  diffraction limit in order to measure  the temperature of nanoscale hotspots  and so there's a number of ways that we  try to do that and one possibility and  the one that i'll focus on today  is that of using a single nanoparticle  with temperature dependent luminescence  in order to measure the temperature of  these nanoscale hotspots  basic idea is that we take a single  nanoparticle  we place it on some sample of interest  we use a laser to excite the  nanoparticle  and then we measure the temperature  dependent luminescence emitted by the  nanoparticle in order to determine the  temperature of our sample  the idea here is that the nanoparticle  is very small in an excellent thermal  contact with the sample such that it  accurately reports the true temperature  of the sample  and now i just want to drill in for a  moment on how we're getting nano scale  temperature information even though  we're using a diffraction limited laser  and so it's true that the diameter of  our excitation laser beam is indeed  diffraction limited  but the key is that if we are only  exciting a single nanoparticle  then we know that the temperature  information comes only from the  nanoparticle luminescence and therefore  the spatial resolution of the  temperature measurement is governed by  our nanoparticle size rather than the  diffraction one of the laser spots  and so when we perform our experiments  we ensure that the nanoparticles are  spaced sufficiently far apart on average  such that their diffraction limited  emission spots don't overlap  and so what that means is that we can't  math temperature with this approach but  we can get really excellent single point  temperature measurements and that's  exactly what we need for these nanoscale  hotspot applications  and so going forward i'll talk about  these specific nanoparticles  that we use the thermometry signals that  we've applied  and finally how we're able to place a  single nanoparticle at the exact  location where we want to measure the  temperature  and these are all important steps  towards actually measuring the  temperature  of one of these nanoscale hot spots like  the hammer peg which was a motivating  application for much of this work  so next we'll talk about the specific  type of nanoparticles that we use and  how we perform our temperature  measurements  we work with what are known as up  converting nanoparticles and there are  many different types but they all  typically consist of a fluoride or an  oxide host matrix doped with a  combination of lanthanide ions  the canonical upconverting nanoparticle  composition and the one that we use  quite frequently is a sodium atrium  fluoride host dosed with a combination  of ytterbium and erbium ions  they're called up converting because the  sensitizer ion which in this case is the  ytterbium absorbs two low-energy  near-infrared photons transfers this  energy to the erbium which finally emits  a single photon and the visible  wavelength range  and these upconverting nanoparticles or  ucnp's have a lot of advantages that  make them popular for a variety of  imaging and sensing applications  one advantage is that they're extremely  photos stable so they don't photo bleach  or blink  bleaching and blinking are big problems  for some probes like quantum dots so  ucnp's are an appealing alternative  ucnp's are also easy to synthesize using  high throughput combinatorial methods  they're also highly tunable so just by  swapping out one dopant for another or  changing the dopant concentration you  can get a big variation in parameters  like emission wavelengths  and finally ucnp's are highly  biocompatible in part because many of  them are excited in the first or second  biological windows which are regions  where tissue is relatively transparent  and so for this reason the bioimaging  community has led a lot of their  development  but the reason that we're interested in  ucnp's is that they have a particular  temperature dependent emission feature  so as i mentioned a moment ago the up  conversion process begins when the  ytterbium absorbs two near-infrared  photons and then transfers this energy  uh to the erbium  next the erbium decays non-radiatively  to a lower energy excited state before  returning to the ground state by  emitting a single green photon  this is clearly a complicated system  with many different energy levels and  transitions  but we can really understand the basic  temperature dependent physics by  considering a simple three level diagram  for erbium  so what you see here is erbium's ground  state along with two higher energy  thermally coupled excited states from  which the erbium can return to the  ground state by emitting a green photon  and the key is that the relative  population of these two excited states  is governed by boltzmann statistics and  that's fundamentally where our  temperature dependent signal comes from  the relative  populations of these two excited states  it turns out are directly related to the  emission intensities  from the two corresponding transitions  and we can see that by looking at single  particle spectra taken at different  temperatures  so if we divide the emission wavelength  range into two bands lambda 1 to lambda  2 and lambda 2 to lambda 3  we see that when we increase the  temperature  the emission intensity from this first  wavelength band which corresponds to the  transition from the higher energy  excited state to the ground state  increases relative to the emission  intensity from the second band which  corresponds to the transition from the  lower energy excited state to the ground  state  we can then define a temperature  dependent ratio as the integrated  emission intensity from the first  wavelength band divided by the  integrated intensity from the second  band  and this is an approach known as  ratiometric thermometry  it's been used quite commonly for  ensembles or large groups of these  upconverting nanoparticles but prior to  our work a single nanoparticle  measurements were quite rare and there  were none that use single particles  under 50 nanometers  next i'll just discuss briefly how we  perform these measurements and then show  some of the results  so we perform these measurements using a  custom scanning confocal microscopy  system and we begin by focusing a  near-infrared laser using a high n a  objective and this focused laser beam  then excites a single nanoparticle which  sits on both a  piezo control nano positioning stage and  a temperature controlled thermal stage  we then collect the emitted luminescence  filter out any residual excitation light  and then we can send that emitted  luminescence either to an avalanche  photodiode for imaging or to a  spectrometer with a ccd camera  and so here's an image of a  microscopy system that we constructed  here in my lab at the university of  rochester  this plot shows the ratio as a function  of temperature for five individual 20 by  40 nanometer  particles which were confirmed to be  single particles using sem imaging  we can see that the temperature  sensitivity of this technique is quite  good about 1500 ppm per kelvin and  there's also an excellent particle to  particle uniformity which you can see  from the nearly overlapping calibration  curves four or five different particles  and just for perspective  again this was the first time that  particles below 50 nanometers have been  used for thermometry and this is about  20x smaller than the  previously smallest  single particles that had been used for  isolated single particle measurements  like we're doing and about 5x smaller  than the  smallest single particle measurements  for a particle that was attached to an  afm tip and so this really represents a  significant improvement in terms of the  spatial resolution of this technique  all right so next i'll talk about what  began as an effort to answer the common  and important question of whether our  excitation laser could be  heating the nanoparticles but it  ultimately  morphed into a more complicated story  that led us to uncover some important  effects that occur when you excise these  nanoparticles at very high excitation  intensities  which has some important practical  uh  impacts in terms of this thermometry  technique  so if we look at the literature data for  nanoparticle ensembles it's been shown  that this temperature dependent ratio is  essentially independent of excitation  intensity  but because single nanoparticle  measurements are as i said much more  rare there haven't been the same kinds  of systematic studies looking at how the  ratio varies with intensity  but if we look at the typical excitation  intensity is required for single  nanoparticles versus unsaw bulbs  we see that they're quite different  so specifically single nanoparticles  require much higher excitation  intensities by several orders of  magnitude  and so it's been natural to ask could  these high excitation intensities  required for single nanoparticles affect  this ratiometric thermometry signal  and here i want to discuss two different  possibilities  so one possibility is that the high  excitation intensity could increase the  temperature  um  of the nanoparticle which is what we  refer to as self-heating  the second possibility is that the high  excitation intensity could distort the  boltzmann population distribution  that our ratiometric thermometry  technique relies on even though the  nanoparticle temperature hasn't changed  and so the second case uh seemed  unlikely uh based on the precedent in  the literature and so we began by  looking into this first case to see if  the high laser excitation intensities  could be heating the nanoparticle  and so we begin by first making what i  call a worst case  scenario estimate so in other words an  estimate of the highest possible  temperature rise that we could expect  for our nanoparticles and i'll  illustrate that by building up a thermal  circuit here  so we start by assuming that all of the  laser light absorbed by the nanoparticle  is converted to heat although in reality  a small amount is up converted to higher  energy light  and we calculate the absorption  cross-section based on the well-known  cross-section for eterbium ions which  are the primary absorbers of the 980  nanometer excitation light  and then this heat can either be  dissipated on the um error side  which we represent uh let's see so sorry  shown here is the substrate side  so on the substrate side we represent  that with two different thermal  resistors one representing the thermal  contact resistance between the  nanoparticle and the substrate and then  another resistor representing the  substrate thermal resistance  and then our other parallel  path is that heat could be dissipated on  the air side which we represent with  this thermal resistor our air  so again we want the worst case scenario  the highest possible temperature rise  that we could expect for this  nanoparticle  and so to get that what we do is we  ignore the substrate size thermal  resistors and we only consider our air  um and so this isn't necessarily the  most realistic scenario in fact based on  our estimates of the magnitudes of these  resistors we expect that a lot of the  heat actually will flow through the  substrate side  but it is the most conservative the  reason for that is that our air is the  largest thermal resistor and so if we  only allow the heat to flow out of the  particle through the air that will give  us the highest possible temperature rise  we could expect  and so for a 50 nanometer particle which  corresponds to those that we use in this  next set of experiments we calculate a  value of about two times ten to the nine  kelvins per watt for our air  and i'll refer to this value as our  model from here on out  and for an excitation intensity of 3  times 10 to the 5 watts per centimeter  squared which corresponds to the upper  end of the range that we use in this  next set of experiments  we calculate a temperature rise of about  2.1 kelvin  uh so not too bad even in this worst  case scenario estimate we're only  getting a few kelvin temperature rise  um and finally just to familiarize you  with a format that i'll be using  repeatedly in this next section here you  see the temperature rise the function of  excitation intensity and the slope of  this line is directly related to the  thermal resistance and the y-axis here  goes up to 50 kelvin just to emphasize  that if we're interested in measuring  temperature changes on that order then  even this worst case scenario 2 kelvin  self-heating isn't a total deal breaker  however when we actually started  performing experiments where we measured  the ratio of excitation intensity  we saw something that was different than  what we expected  so here you see the ratio measured as a  function of excitation intensity  where our substrate temperature is fixed  at room temperature about 296 kelvin  and we see that as we increase the  excitation intensity that temperature  dependent ratio also increases  likewise if we park our thermal stage at  a higher temperature of 350 kelvin and  we again increase the excitation ratio  uh the excitation intensity  we once again see that this temperature  dependent ratio increases  so what we can then do is to convert  this change in the ratio with excitation  intensity to an apparent temperature  rise  and if we do that we get an apparent  temperature rise  of over 50 kelvin so much much higher  than our worst case scenario estimate of  just 2 kelvin  and from this data we can also extract  an apparent thermal resistance and when  we do that we get a value of about 6.3  times 10 to the 10 kelvins per watt  so again more than an order of magnitude  higher than the worst case scenario  modeled value  and so it's really this dramatic  discrepancy between model and experiment  that's motivated the next set of  experiments we performed to try and  answer the question of could the  nanoparticle really be heating and if so  how can we explain this  so after observing this unexpectedly  large apparent temperature rise one of  our first thoughts was to next try to  use a second thermometry method  to verify  uh this large temperature rise and so  for the sake of time i won't go into  details but we developed a second  thermometry method based on the  temperature dependent luminescence  lifetime of these nanoparticles  and so we use this method to once again  measure the apparent temperature rise as  a function of excitation intensity  and once again we got a quite large  apparent temperature rise of somewhere  around 50 kelvin and so the second  thermometry method seemed to confirm  that the nanoparticle was perhaps  heating up by  you know far more than our conservative  model i would have suggested  and we can also extract an apparent  thermal resistance from the  lifetime thermometry method and it's  similar to the value that we got from  our original ratio metric thermometry  measurements  now one thing that these two experiments  both have in common is that the only  parameter we're varying is the  excitation intensity  and there are scenarios we can imagine  for example if we were really off in  terms of our  estimate of the absorption cross-section  where the temperature rise  that we estimate would also be quite  lost  but even if our estimates for the  magnitude of the temperature rise were  totally incorrect  if we started somehow changing these  thermal resistors  so somehow manipulating the thermal  environment surrounding the nanoparticle  this would have to have a noticeable  impact on any true temperature rise  and so that's what we did next  we started by repeating our measurements  on three different ir transparent  substrates with thermal conductivity  spanning over two orders of magnitude  namely  a polymer glass and sapphire substrates  and when we did that we measured a  essentially identical apparent  temperature rise in all three cases  and so that told us that the substrate  thermal resistance did not seem to  affect the heat dissipation  now if you recall we actually  thought that we should expect a lot of  heat to be dissipated through the  substrate side and so next we decided to  try varying the contact resistance to  see if the enlarged thermal contact  resistance between the nanoparticle and  the substrate could be limiting the heat  dissipation  all right so to vary the thermal contact  resistances we applied five different  coatings to our samples designed to  improve the thermal coupling between the  nanoparticle and the substrate so these  coatings were spin coated and drop cast  polystyrene  we applied hsq which is a spin on glass  sweatered sao2 and finally a thomas  layer deposition of sao2  the ald in particular ensures highly  formal deposition since it uses gas  phase precursors  nonetheless we measured essentially the  same apparent temperature rise for all  these coatings  and so we concluded that the contact  resistance does not  impact the  heat dissipation  and now it turns out that if you think a  bit more carefully  uh changing the contact resistance is a  little bit complicated because doing so  actually changes the air thermal  resistance as well  so i won't go into all the details for  the sake of time  but by taking that into consideration we  were able to conclude  that none of the thermal resistors  affected the heat dissipation  so the last thing that we tried is that  we tried changing the absorption cross  section of our nanoparticles and so we  chose to do that by varying the  nanoparticle  size while keeping the concentration  of the eterbium ions constant but with a  larger size nanoparticle you get more  eterbium ions and you increase the  absorption cross-section  so what we found is that for both a 20  by 40 nanometer particle and a 50  nanometer particle we measured again the  same apparent temperature rise  and this just cannot be explained by any  heat transfer model  the reason for that is that these  temperature rise is equal to the heat  absorbed q times the thermal resistance  r  we know that q scales with the  nanoparticle diameter d cubed  and so to have  the temperature rise theta be  independent of c the thermal resistance  r would have to scale as 1 over d cubed  and so it turns out that we're right in  the middle of  being right on the border of being in a  classical fourier heat transfer regime  versus a ballistic heat transfer regime  going to something i mentioned at the  beginning  of this seminar  but in either case we can't get that one  over d cubed scaling we might be  somewhere between the one over d scaling  uh from the fourier case or one over d  squared from the ballistic case but  there's just no way that we can get that  one over d cubed scaling  and so we concluded that the absorption  cross section and the particle diameter  don't affect the heat dissipation  so just to recap what i've shown up to  this point first our worst case scenario  self-heating estimate predicted no more  than a few kelvin of heating  and then we found that the apparent  self-heating that we measured despite  being 50 kelvin or more  didn't seem to depend on any formal  parameters we tried varying all these  resistors and the absorption cross  section but saw no changes  and so at that point we really had to  ask is there any way that this effect  could in fact be non-thermal and even  though this did not seem like a strong  possibility early on at this point our  experiments clearly said that this could  not be a true temperature rise  and so my collaborators did some more  modeling to  really investigate in more detail what  might be going on  so i'm fortunate to have some great  collaborators dr emory chan lawrence  berkeley lab and dr ayala titleboyne  who was also at lawrence berkeley lab at  the time  and they've developed some very  sophisticated rate equation models um  that let them  uh  essentially calculate  all the different uh you know absorption  and transition and energy transfer and  luminescence mechanisms that are going  on in these nanoparticles  and from these calculations they're able  to actually  uh model  the ratios that we measure  uh so what you can see here is the  energy level diagram for erbium that i  showed before  and i only showed energy levels up to a  certain point because that's typically  the range that's considered important  for ratiometric thermometry  but in reality there are a whole bunch  of higher energy excited states and at  high excitation intensities these higher  energy excited states can actually  become significantly populated  and so in their models  the collaborators account for a whole  bunch of different higher energy excited  states and transitions involving those  states and so the yellow boxes that you  see in these two diagrams  enclose the thermally coupled levels  that give rise to  the emission that we use to calculate  the temperature dependent ratio and then  everything above that on the right hand  diagram  is a higher energy excited state  and so again through a detailed  accounting of all the ways in which  absorption emission  energy transfer non-radiative relaxation  and so on can occur  my collaborators are able to calculate  the ratio as a function of excitation  intensity  when they did that they found  surprisingly that their models actually  predict that the ratio will increase  with excitation intensity  uh the model values are just a bit lower  than what we measure experimentally but  we don't expect an exact quantitative  match since these models use many  semi-empirical parameters  so we can then do is to plot the  relative increase and the modeled ratio  values by normalizing those values with  respect to their minimum  and we can then plot analogous  experimental data  and what we see is that there's quite a  good match between the measured and  modeled a relative increase in the ratio  and so again i'll just emphasize that  there are no free parameters that we use  to get this match between the measured  and modeled relative increase in this  ratio  uh finally by looking at the modeling  results in a bit more detail  we see that it's a combination  of  radiative and non-radiative relaxation  from higher energy excited states in  erbium that distorts the boltzmann  population distribution our ratiometric  thermometry technique relies on and  increases the ratio  and so of course it's important to be  aware of this kind of non-thermal change  on the ratio but once we really  understand this kind of effect in detail  we can calibrate it or work at low  excitation intensities and still use  this technique as a way to measure  nanoscale hotspots  lastly just to really confirm  that this effect was truly non-thermal  we did some final experiments where we  coated  some glass  samples substrates with a thin layer of  platinum and then placed nanoparticles  on these samples  the reason to do that is that the  platinum which is pretty absorptive in  the near ir  will therefore absorb a decent amount of  the laser light but then we have this  low thermal conductivity glass layer  underneath  that really  is not a good heat dissipator and so  what that means is that we can get a  temperature rise of  hundreds of degrees when we  shine a laser onto this type of sample  so what we did is we first started out  by using just a plain glass sample so no  platinum on this one and we measure the  ratio as a function of excitation  intensity and unsurprisingly we see an  increase in the ratio and that's this  apparent self-heating effect that i just  talked about  um and then if we repeat this  measurement with a nanoparticle on  platinum coated glass  then what happens is we get a much much  steeper increase in the ratio indicative  of true laser heating on top of this  apparent self-heating effect  and we also lose the signal from the  nanoparticle at certain points in terms  of increasing the excitation intensity  and that's consistent with other reports  in the literature as these nanoparticles  degrade around 600 kelvin or so  we then repeated this experiment  for  diamond substrates which has a much much  higher thermal conductivity by orders of  magnitude  and here we see that the results are the  same whether it's for a nanoparticle on  their glass bare diamond or platinum  coated diamond and so that makes sense  for a non-thermal  effect because in all those cases  there is no heating going on and this  increase in the ratio of excitation  intensity is a purely non-thermal effect  lastly before wrapping up here i just  want to answer the question of how we're  able to take a single one of these  nanoparticles and place it at the exact  location on the sample where we want to  measure temperature that's of course  very critical because if we don't have a  way to do that then this whole technique  really falls apart  and so the approach that we take here is  that we use an atomic force microscope  tip or afn tip  to push individual nanoparticles around  substrates and place them at the  location where we'd like to measure  temperature  so the way that we do that and a lot of  this work right now is being done by my  graduate student dinesh committee  is that we essentially hack the nano  lithography mode on our park nx10 afm  and then we use that to position  individual nanoparticles uh on various  solid substrates  and so the examples that i'll show here  are for  individual  nanodiamonds  that are about 100 nanometers or even  less in diameter but again we apply this  technique quite broadly to all kinds of  nanoparticles  uh so we can do both microscale  manipulation which is how dinesh made  this r for rochester that you see shown  here  and then we can also do finer movements  so here's an example where we move one  particle with respect to another  by about 95 nanometers  and then here's another example of  moving a single particle by just about  20 nanometers so this provides a  powerful way to place single  nanoparticles exactly where we want to  perform our temperature measurements  so to wrap up in my group we really  focus on pushing the boundaries of  far-field optical nanothermometry  uh in the past we've done a lot of work  on using individual upconverting  nanoparticles for sub 50 nanometer  single point thermometry and that's  really the majority of the work that i  talked about today  uh and then we have some other projects  in slightly different directions at the  moment  finally i'd like to acknowledge uh past  funding support from uc berkeley and the  nsf  uh the molecular foundry at lawrence  berkeley lab and seagate and then  current funding support from the  university of rochester and the american  chemical society petroleum research fund  um if you're interested in learning more  please feel free to visit our website  listed here at the bottom of the slide  and then with that i'd be happy to  answer any questions  thank you andrea for your informative  presentation we'll now start the live q  a portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on that ask a question  box located on the far left of your  screen we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  let's get started  our first question  um how do the up converting  nanoparticles compare to other  fluorescent therm thermometers  uh sure so that's a great question  because certainly these upconverting  nanoparticles are not the only types of  luminescent materials that have been  used for thermometry  there's a lot of things like fluorescent  dyes proteins that have been used for  thermometry as well as other inorganic  materials  so for example nitrogen vacancy centers  and nanodiamonds and we work with some  of these other materials as well  so i think some of the major advantages  of the upconverting nanoparticle would  be first of all they're just extremely  uniform  and i talked about that a bit when i  showed some of these ratio metric  thermometry's results for different  individual particles and you see that  these calibration curves are really  uniform  from one particle to another so that's a  big advantage in terms of being able to  do say a batch calibration where you  calibrate a small number of single  particles  from a batch and then you can go on and  apply that calibration to a totally  different nanoparticle from that same  batch  so that's one big advantage  another  aspect that i'd highlight is  because you excite these upconverted  nanoparticles in the near ir but then  they emit  hundreds of nanometers away in the  visible wavelength range that can be a  big advantage if you're trying to avoid  a spectral overlap with some other kind  of signal  and that's such a huge anti-stokes shift  that you really don't get from a lot of  other types of materials  and so that can be  you know a unique advantage as well  thank you for that andreas our next  question  if you were to use a single up  converting nanoparticle as a thermometer  in a plasmonic environment such as the  hammer hard drive we mentioned would you  expect expect this plasmonic environment  to change the ratiometric thermometry  signal  sure so yeah that's an important  question right because we know that for  something like that hammer hard drive  application you're going to need to  put these nanoparticles in a very  intense plasmonic environment and it's  definitely reasonable  to worry that you know perhaps this  plasmonic environment could change the  temperature dependent luminescence  signal  and so one thing that's helpful is that  there are other people who have already  used these upconverting nanoparticles  for thermometry in plasmonic  environments  and they actually see that  the ratio values that you get as a  function of temperature  are extremely similar to what you get in  more of a conventional non-plasmonic  environment  so that's kind of a helpful precedent  there  you get some enhancement in the overall  brightness which is actually  a big advantage perhaps but you don't  see distortions  of the ratio  the other thing that can be helpful and  this goes back to what i was saying  previously about having this advantage  of a large separation between  the excitation wavelength and the  emission wavelength and then also the  fact that these wavelengths are just  very tunable  uh by changing these lanthanide dopants  is that you can  basically ensure that your excitation  wavelengths and your emission  wavelengths are both not anywhere near  the plasmonic resonance  and so that's something that you know we  were thinking about when we selected the  particular nanoparticles that we use for  this work in order to try and avoid  any interference with that plasmonic  aspect of these hammer drives  great thank you looks like we have time  for one more question what are some  potential ways to mitigate the  non-thermal excite excitation intensity  artifacts that you discussed and what  are the broader implications of this  type of artifact  sure um  right so as i talked about during the  presentation you know these excitation  intensity artifacts if you're not aware  of them can potentially uh really  distort your temperature measurements  which is of course  not ideal  so i think there are a few ways that you  can get around it so  one is just to work at a fixed  excitation intensity rate so if you can  stay at a stable excitation intensity  value throughout your measurements  then you're not going to have to worry  about these kinds of effects showing up  a kind of similar idea is to work at low  excitation intensities where even their  small changes in the excitation  intensity will not give you this big  apparent temperature change so that's  another way  um to avoid these effects  but then even if you're in a situation  where you know you need to work at  higher excitation intensities and  perhaps you cannot guarantee that the  excitation intensity is held constant uh  just being aware of these effects you  can calibrate them you know by doing the  excitation intensity sweeps um that we  did  and then you know take that into account  so that you understand what is really a  temperature change and what is this kind  of non-thermal um artifact  um so in terms of you know broader  implications i think that it's something  to consider in the context of libsyn's  nanothermometry more broadly which as i  mentioned if you know you know a lot of  people  are using uh with many different  materials which is great uh certainly i  think it's a really powerful  technique you know at the same time it's  important to be aware that many of these  signals that have been identified as  temperature dependent uh can also change  due to other parameters you know whether  it's something like excitation intensity  or  you know things like pressure for  example  uh there's a lot of reasons these  signals can change and so i think just  doing these very careful experiments  to really tease out what are temperature  changes what is something else  is probably important more broadly for  the field of fluorescence thermometry  thank you again andrea for your time  today and your important research we'd  also like to thank lab roots and our  sponsor andor for writing underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  any questions we do not have time for  today and those submitted during the  on-demand period will be addressed by  the speaker via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues and anyone who have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

